---
/**
 * TweetCard — a saved x.com post, as X renders it, with a way back to the
 * notes underneath.
 *
 * **This shipped as a static card first and Aayush reversed it.** The card was
 * react-tweet's anatomy rebuilt from files in this repository — a monogram in
 * place of the avatar, the words, a media grid, no script, no request. Every
 * argument for it still holds and it still loads nothing; what it could not do
 * was look right. His review of the shipped wall: the cards look kind of odd.
 * A hand-built imitation of a tweet is uncanny in the way an imitation of
 * anything familiar is, and no amount of matching their CSS closes that,
 * because what is missing is the avatar and the marks a copy cannot fetch.
 *
 * So the card is now the official embed, and the thing that used to be the
 * whole card is the **fallback inside the blockquote**: what a reader sees
 * before X's factory arrives, what they see for ever with scripting off, and
 * the height the masonry column reserves in the meantime.
 * `components/XEmbeds.astro` carries what that costs and who decided it.
 *
 * ## Two anchors now, and that is the rule changing rather than breaking
 *
 * The old card was one link on purpose: a press anywhere on it went to one
 * place, so it could never be a coin toss. An embed cannot keep that — every
 * link inside X's iframe goes to X, and none of them is ours to redirect. So
 * the destination this site cares about moves **outside** the embed, into a bar
 * under it that says `notes` and goes to `/library/<slug>`.
 *
 * That is the video tile's arrangement rather than a new one: two regions with
 * two obvious affordances, press the thing and you get the thing, press the
 * label and you get the page about it. What the card must never be is one
 * region with two meanings, and it is not.
 *
 * The permalink at the foot of the fallback is the third anchor and it is only
 * ever seen when the embed has not loaded. It is what X's factory reads the
 * post's id out of, and it wears the `.ext` arrow for the reason the row's
 * `source` link does: it is the one control here that ends the visit.
 *
 * ## One box, and the two regions live inside it
 *
 * **The bar used to be a second box under a first one, and Aayush's review of
 * the shipped wall is what closed them up.** The fallback carried the border
 * and the `notes` bar carried its own, so a hydrated card was X's rounded frame
 * with an unrelated bordered strip below it — two objects that happened to be
 * stacked rather than one card with two parts. The border is on `.card` now and
 * the bar keeps a `border-top` as the rule *between* the regions, which is the
 * site's one way of separating things (design.md §1) doing the separating from
 * the inside.
 *
 * **And the whole box is the way in, except for the embed.** `.card__link`
 * stretches an `::before` across `.card`, so the ring of card around X's frame
 * and the bar under it all open `/library/<slug>`; `.card__embed` sits above it
 * on `z-index: 1`, so every link inside X's iframe still goes to X and the
 * fallback's own words are still selectable and its `source` link still
 * pressable. An `<a>` could not have been wrapped round the blockquote to do
 * this — nesting the embed inside an anchor would swallow X's own links, which
 * are the reader's whole reason for looking at an embed rather than a quote.
 *
 * The keyboard gets the real anchor and nothing else: the bar is the focusable
 * control, the stretched pseudo carries no tab stop of its own, and the focus
 * ring lands on the bar rather than round the whole card, which is where a
 * reader's eye already is when they arrive by Tab.
 *
 * ## What the fallback still refuses
 *
 * No avatar, no brand mark, no `pbs.twimg.com`: the pictures are files in this
 * repository and `lib/library.ts › readShotPath` makes that structural rather
 * than careful — the parser will not carry a remote URL, so no fallback can
 * render one. The monogram is still the avatar slot with a letter in it. All of
 * that is about what *this site* fetches before X's factory runs, and it is
 * still nothing.
 *
 * The text is one paragraph because that is the shape it arrives in: the
 * pipeline flattens a post's markdown, so `Post.text` carries no newline to
 * preserve. It is cut at `POST_CARD_MAX` for the reason `lib/post.ts` gives at
 * length, which now applies only to the fallback — X's own embed decides where
 * to cut a long post inside its frame.
 */
import type { LibraryEntry, Post } from "../lib/library";
import { entryHref } from "../lib/library";
import { hueSlot } from "../lib/tags";
import { POST_CARD_MAX, clipText, monogram } from "../lib/post";

interface Props {
  /** The entry the card links to. Its `post` is what the card draws. */
  entry: LibraryEntry & { post: Post };
}

const { entry } = Astro.props;
const { post } = entry;

/** Where a /library thing sends a reader, shared with the row and the tile. */
const href = entryHref(entry);

const initial = monogram(post.author);
const body = clipText(post.text, POST_CARD_MAX);
---

<li class="card press">
  {/*
    The embed's own region, and the wrapper is load-bearing twice over. X's
    factory inserts its rendered widget immediately before the blockquote it
    consumed, so putting a box round the blockquote is what puts a box round
    the widget too — one element to lift above the stretched link, whichever
    of the two is currently on screen. `XEmbeds.astro` reads
    `previousElementSibling`, which is still their widget, so the height watch
    is untouched by the extra level.
  */}
  <div class="card__embed">
    {/*
      `twitter-tweet` is what X's factory looks for and `data-dnt` is the one
      thing we can ask it for: do not use this visit to tailor anything. The
      theme arrives from `XEmbeds.astro` before the factory does, because an
      iframe on another origin cannot be re-themed once it exists.

      Everything inside is the fallback. It is real content rather than a
      placeholder — with scripting off it is the whole card, and until the
      factory lands it is what holds the column open.
    */}
    <blockquote class="card__quote twitter-tweet" data-dnt="true">
      <span class="card__head">
        {
          /*
           * Hidden from the accessibility tree: the letter is a recognition aid
           * for the name sitting beside it, and announcing "B" before "Ben Lang"
           * is a stutter, not a fact. Same call `XGlyph.astro` makes.
           *
           * Absent entirely when the display name has no letter or digit in it,
           * because an empty coloured circle claims an identity it is not
           * carrying (`lib/post.ts › monogram`).
           *
           * **It stays here and it left `PostBody.astro`**, which is not an
           * inconsistency: this is a fallback standing in for a tweet, where the
           * avatar slot is part of the thing being stood in for, and that page
           * is somebody reading a post rather than a picture of one.
           */
          initial !== "" && (
            <span class="monogram" data-hue={hueSlot(post.handle)} aria-hidden="true">
              {initial}
            </span>
          )
        }

        <span class="card__who">
          <span class="card__author">{post.author}</span>
          <span class="card__meta mono">
            <span class="card__handle">@{post.handle}</span>
            <span class="card__date tabular-nums">{post.date}</span>
          </span>
        </span>
      </span>

      <p class="card__text">{body}</p>

      {
        post.media.length > 0 && (
          <span class="card__media" data-count={post.media.length}>
            {post.media.map((src, index) => (
              <img
                class="outlined"
                src={src}
                alt={
                  post.media.length === 1
                    ? `Picture attached to the post by ${post.author}`
                    : `Picture ${index + 1} of ${post.media.length} attached to the post by ${post.author}`
                }
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
            ))}
          </span>
        )
      }

      {/*
        The post itself, and the last anchor in the blockquote on purpose: X's
        factory reads the id out of it. `source` is the word the /library row
        uses for the same control, so a reader who has seen one has seen both,
        and the title rides along where only a screen reader hears it.
      */}
      <a
        class="card__source mono ext"
        href={entry.url}
        rel="noopener nofollow"
        target="_blank"
      >
        source<span class="visually-hidden">: the post by {post.author}</span>
      </a>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  {/*
    Inside the card and outside the embed, which is the whole anatomy. The bar
    is the card's own destination, the only one on it that stays here, and the
    anchor in it stretches across every part of the card the embed is not
    standing on.
  */}
  <p class="card__notes">
    <a class="card__link mono" href={href}>
      notes<span class="visually-hidden"> on {entry.title}</span>
    </a>
  </p>
</li>

<style>
  /* The item in the wall, and **the card itself is the box now**.

     `PostWall.astro` owns the columns; what belongs to a card is that it must
     not be split across one, the air under it, and the border that says the
     embed and the bar under it are one thing. That border used to be on the
     fallback, which meant a hydrated card was X's rounded frame with a
     separately-bordered strip below it — the grouping Aayush's review asked
     for, missing. Sharp corners, design.md §3: a radius marks a token you could
     pick up, never a surface you look through. X's own frame is rounded and
     that is theirs, sitting inside ours.

     `--card-line` is one value read by two borders — this one and the bar's
     rule — so a hover lifts the whole box rather than half of it. It is a
     custom property rather than two declarations for the reason
     `--gap-masthead` is one: a number written twice is a number nobody can
     retune.

     It carries `.press` rather than the bar's anchor doing so, and that is the
     stretched link's doing. An absolutely-positioned `::before` resolves
     `inset: 0` against its nearest *positioned or transformed* ancestor, so a
     transform on the anchor would re-anchor the overlay to the bar mid-press —
     a target that shrinks under a finger already on it. Pressing the box scales
     the box, which is also the truer statement now that the box is the target
     (design.md §4). */
  .card {
    --press-scale: 0.985;
    --card-line: var(--hairline);

    position: relative;
    break-inside: avoid;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--card-line);
    /* Stated rather than left to the initial value, which is what design.md §3
       asks of everything structural: a 4px corner marks a token you could pick
       up, never a surface you look through. */
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  /* The embed's region, and the one part of the card the stretched link does
     not cover.

     `z-index` is what says so. The overlay is a positioned pseudo later in the
     document, so without a layer of its own this box would paint under it and
     every link inside X's iframe would be swallowed by a link to our page —
     which is the one thing an embed may not lose. Lifting it also keeps the
     fallback's words selectable and its `source` link pressable in the seconds
     before the factory lands.

     The inset is what stops two frames touching: X's rounded border sits inside
     our sharp one with the page showing between them, rather than the two
     reading as a double rule. It is also the fallback's inset, which is why the
     blockquote below has none of its own.

     **A margin and not a padding, and the difference is the whole point.**
     Padding is part of this element's box, so the ring of card around X's frame
     would belong to the embed and a press on it would land on nothing — which
     is exactly the "anywhere that is not the embed" this card is supposed to
     answer. Measured before the change: a press 3px inside the card's own
     border hit `.card__embed`. A margin is card, so the ring goes to the
     stretched link with the rest of the box. */
  .card__embed {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    margin: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* The fallback, wearing the card the static version was.

     X hides this element and puts its own iframe beside it once the factory
     runs, so everything below is what a reader sees before that and instead of
     it. No border and no padding of its own: both belong to the card and the
     region above, so the fallback and the embed are inset by the same amount
     and swapping one for the other moves nothing. */
  .card__quote {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    /* The height the masonry column holds while the factory is in flight.
       X's shortest rendered card is a little under this, so a very short post
       reserves roughly what it is about to become instead of collapsing to
       four lines and then jumping. Every post saved here is taller than it
       already, so it costs the current data nothing. */
    min-height: 8rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
  }

  /* The fallback stands down once the embed is **actually there**, and the two
     words doing the work are "actually there".

     X's factory inserts the rendered widget as a sibling and marks the
     blockquote it consumed with a `data-twitter-extracted-<session id>`
     attribute — an attribute name nothing can select on, because the id changes
     per page load — and, measured on the shipped page, leaves it visible. Two
     copies of the same post, one ours and one theirs, stacked. So we hide it
     ourselves.

     **This was keyed on `.twitter-tweet-rendered` and that was wrong, on the
     live page, for as long as it took their iframes to load.** Their factory
     inserts the container long before the iframe reports a height, so the
     moment the class appeared the readable fallback was thrown away and the
     wall went to nothing: measured on aayushmanchanda.com, twenty-four widgets
     at zero height and the document collapsing from 6311px to 1835px, which is
     a page of `notes` bars with no posts above them. On a load where X never
     sizes them at all — rate limited, blocked, having a bad day — that is the
     final state.

     design.md §3 says the whole point of this dependency's failure modes is
     that they stay visible: **visible and wrong beats silent and wrong**. A
     class name is not evidence that an embed rendered; a height is. So the
     hide is keyed on `data-embedded`, which `XEmbeds.astro` puts on this
     blockquote only once the widget beside it has one, and takes off again if
     it loses it.

     That also drops an assumption rather than adding one. The class name is no
     longer load-bearing anywhere; the only thing left is that their widget
     lands immediately before the blockquote it consumed, and that assumption
     now lives in the script where it can be checked, failing the same harmless
     way it always did — the fallback stays visible under the embed. */
  .card__quote[data-embedded] {
    display: none;
  }

  .card__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .card__who {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .card__author {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .card__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  /* Drawn, never typed into the content: design.md §6. Keyed to the item after
     it, the same way the footer's colophon does it, so a wrapped line starts
     with the separator instead of the line above ending on a dangling one. The
     `margin-right` matches the flex gap on its other side, and the colour is
     the one every separator on the site takes. */
  .card__handle + .card__date::before {
    content: "\00b7" / "";
    margin-right: 0.4rem;
    color: var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  .card__date {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .card__text {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted);
    /* A post arrives flattened to one paragraph, so nothing here has to survive
       a newline. `overflow-wrap` is for the other thing a post is full of: a
       t.co shortlink with no word boundary in it, which would otherwise run out
       past the card's own border. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The quiet pattern, the same one `LibraryList.astro › .row__source` takes:
     `--muted` because it is a control and design.md §4 is the floor, and its
     own underline because on touch there is no hover to reveal one. */
  .card__source {
    align-self: flex-start;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--hairline-strong);
    transition-property: color, text-decoration-color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  .card__source:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }

  /* The way back to this site, and the one thing on the card that survives
     hydration. **A rule inside the card now, rather than a box under it**: it
     was a bordered strip with its own margin, which read as a second object
     stacked on the embed instead of the other half of one card. A `border-top`
     in the card's own `--card-line` is the site's one way of separating things
     (design.md §1) doing it from the inside.

     40px on the nose, which is design.md §4's floor taken literally rather than
     through an `::before`. The stated text exception is for a word in a row of
     words; this is the only affordance on the card that stays on the site, on a
     page that is a wall of other people's frames, and a control that has to be
     obvious does not get to be a hint. It is also inside the 46px the thebridge
     reference sets its own list rows at. */
  .card__notes {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--card-line);
    /* The card carries `.press`, which already names `border-color` for its own
       border; this rule is a second border on a different element and needs its
       own so the two lift together. One property, named, never `all`
       (design.md §4). */
    transition-property: border-color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  .card__link {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    min-height: 40px;
    padding-inline: 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    /* `.press` used to carry this. It moved to the card, so the ink names its
       own transition — one property, never `all`. */
    transition-property: color;
    transition-duration: var(--dur-fast);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease);
  }

  /* **The card is the target, and this is what makes it one.** Everything in
     the box that the embed is not standing on — the ring of card around X's
     frame, and the bar itself — opens `/library/<slug>`.

     It is a pseudo on the anchor rather than an `<a>` wrapped round the card
     for a reason that is not stylistic: the blockquote becomes X's iframe, and
     an iframe inside an anchor would hand every link in somebody else's post to
     our own destination. The embed has to stay pressable on its own terms, so
     the overlay goes *under* it (`.card__embed › z-index`) instead of round it.

     `inset: 0` resolves against `.card`, which is the nearest positioned
     ancestor: `.card__notes` is deliberately left static so this reaches the
     whole box rather than the bar it lives in. No `z-index` of its own — a
     positioned pseudo already paints above the card's own background, and the
     one thing it must lose to says so explicitly. */
  .card__link::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* Drawn rather than typed, and the alt-text form so it has no accessible
     name of its own: the link is already called `notes on <title>`, and an
     arrow read out after it is furniture being announced. Not the `.ext` arrow,
     which means the visit ends — this one goes one page further in. */
  .card__link::after {
    content: "\2192" / "";
  }

  /* The box lifts when the box is the target, which is now a `:has()` and used
     not to need one. While the border sat on the bar, hovering the bar was the
     only thing that could reach it. The border is on the card now and the card
     is bigger than its target — the embed is inside it and goes somewhere else
     entirely — so a plain `.card:hover` would light the whole box up for a
     pointer resting on an iframe that will not take it anywhere. Keying it to
     the anchor is what keeps the lit border a promise about where a press
     lands, and the anchor's `::before` is most of the box, so nearly all of a
     hover over the card still lights it.

     Not gated behind `@media (hover: hover)` — pressing this unloads the page,
     so the touch latch design.md §4 gates for is gone before it could be
     seen. */
  .card:has(.card__link:hover) {
    --card-line: var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  .card__link:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* The media grid, react-tweet's proportions.

     2px is their gap and it is the right number: any more and four pictures
     read as four pictures rather than as one attachment. The box is reserved by
     `aspect-ratio` rather than by width/height attributes, because nothing here
     knows a picture's size at build time (design.md §8).

     **8:5 is not a new number.** It is what `ShotFrame.astro › .preview__shot`
     already crops an unknown-sized picture to on a card, and a second answer to
     one question is how a site ends up with two crops that nearly match. It
     also happens to be the ratio that makes a 2x2 read as four landscape cells
     rather than four slots.

     Four is as many pictures as x.com lets a post carry, so 1, 2, 3 and 4 are
     the whole vocabulary. A fifth would take a third row and squeeze all of
     them inside the reserved box, which is the honest failure: it shows
     everything, badly, instead of hiding one. */
  .card__media {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    grid-auto-rows: 1fr;
    gap: 2px;
    aspect-ratio: 8 / 5;
    /* The fallback is a column flex container, so this box's automatic minimum
       height is its content — which is the reserved box's own contents, so the
       ratio above would compute a height and then be overruled by it. Zeroing
       the minimum is what lets `aspect-ratio` be the answer. The `min-height`
       on the pictures below is the same rule one level in, for the rows. */
    min-height: 0;
  }

  .card__media[data-count="1"] {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  /* The L: the first picture holds the left column at full height and the other
     two stack beside it. One rule, because the grid places the rest itself. */
  .card__media[data-count="3"] > :first-child {
    grid-row: span 2;
  }

  .card__media img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* A grid item's automatic minimum size is its content, so a portrait
       screenshot in a `1fr` row pushes the row past the reserved box and the
       `aspect-ratio` above quietly stops meaning anything. **This shipped
       first**: four 1200px-tall shots came out 484px deep in a box asked for
       208. Zeroing both minimums is what lets `1fr` be a fraction. */
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* Anchored to the top, the same call and the same reason as
       `ShotFrame.astro › .preview__shot`: what gets attached to a post is
       usually a screenshot or a chart, and the top of one is the half that
       says what it is. */
    object-position: top center;
    /* Sharp, like every other frame on the site. */
    border-radius: 0;
  }
</style>
